You check one object from OpenStreetMap near a property in Berlin, before anyone searches the
web for it. Decide whether its map data, with what you know about it, already settle what it is
and whether it is in use.

The object is clear only if all of these hold:
- Its name and tags, with what you know about it, settle its type: one of the types below,
  which fits it closely. If no type fits it closely, it is not clear.
- Its tags settle its status: operating, unless a tag says that it is disused, abandoned or
  closed. A tag that says that it is under construction or proposed makes it not clear.
- Nothing conflicts: not its name with its tags, not one tag with another, and not the map data
  with what you know. A name that calls a site former, while its tags show it in use, is a
  conflict. So is an object that you know has closed, or is something other than its tags say.

The status comes only from the tags, never from what you know: what you know can only show a
conflict. If anything is missing or conflicts, the object is not clear: it will be investigated
on the web, so a doubt costs little.

